  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 PM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight... Scattered showers and storms which developed \nacross Okeechobee and the Treasure Coast are spreading into \nportions of Brevard and Osceola counties this afternoon. Despite \nslightly drier air further north, light southerly steering flow \nshould continue to shift isolated to scattered coverage towards \nthe Orlando Metro into the evening. The presence of dry air (DCAPE\n~800-1,000 J/kg) and winds aloft (40kts @ 32,000 ft) will allow \nfor a few stronger storms capable of downburst wind gusts 40-55 \nmph and frequent lightning. Quick rainfall accumulations of 2-3\" \nwill also be possible in stronger storms. Ahead of showers and \nstorms, conditions remain hot and humid as temperatures climb \nthrough the 90s, and a Heat Advisory continues through 8 PM. Dry \nconditions are forecast overnight with low temperatures mostly in \nthe mid to upper 70s.\n\nSunday... A mid to upper level ridge holds over the southern U.S. \nwhile surface high pressure extends over the Florida peninsula. A \nlayer of dry air modeled above 700mb will keep rain chances well \nbelow normal with low rain chances (~20%) mostly limited to areas \nfrom Brevard and Osceola counties northward. With little relief from \nafternoon showers and storms, expect hot conditions and above normal \ntemperatures to continue. Highs are forecast mostly in the mid to \nupper 90s across the interior while the sea breeze may be able to \nhold some locations along the immediate coast in the low 90s. \nDespite some mixing down of dry air aloft, peak heat index values \nshould top out near to above Heat Advisory criteria (108F) once \nagain. Continue to practice heat safety and stay hydrated!\n\nMonday-Friday... High pressure broadens aloft into mid week as a \ntrough digs across Canada and into the northern U.S. Higher moisture \ngradually slides southward through the period as a surface boundary \nsinks into the southeast U.S. Isolated to scattered rain chances (20-\n50%) build southward through portions of Brevard and Osceola on \nMonday, with a seasonal pattern of afternoon and evening storms (~40-\n60%) forecast to return areawide by mid to late week. Outside of \nshowers and storms, above normal temperatures should persist. While \nstill abnormally hot, the greatest heat impacts may begin to slowly \nsubside late week as troughing begins to dig through the central and \neastern U.S.",
